  {"id":"ephedrine-dopamine-2","topic":"Ephedrine & dopamine: indirect vasopressors","domain":"Anesthetic pharmacology (inhaled agents, IV induction agents, opioids, neuromuscular blocking agents, local anesthetics, vasopressors, reversal agents)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"What cardiovascular effects are shared between ephedrine and epinephrine?","answer":"Both increase blood pressure, heart rate, contractility, and cardiac output; both cause bronchodilation","rationale":"Ephedrine's combined direct alpha/beta agonism and indirect catecholamine release produces a qualitatively similar profile to epinephrine at lower potency.","bloom":"recall","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":392}],"confusable_with":"ephedrine as pure alpha agonist"},
  {"id":"ephedrine-dopamine-3","topic":"Ephedrine & dopamine: indirect vasopressors","domain":"Anesthetic pharmacology (inhaled agents, IV induction agents, opioids, neuromuscular blocking agents, local anesthetics, vasopressors, reversal agents)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"How does dopamine's receptor activity change with dose?","answer":"Low doses: dopamine-1 receptor vasodilation (renal/splanchnic/cerebral/coronary); higher doses: alpha-adrenergic predominance with vasoconstriction","rationale":"Dopamine's dose-dependent receptor shift from D1 to alpha-1 agonism means its hemodynamic effects transition from vasodilation to vasoconstriction as the dose escalates.","bloom":"analyze","source":[{"book":"Society Guideline","page":29}],"confusable_with":"dopamine uniformly dilates renal vessels at all doses"},
  {"id":"ephedrine-dopamine-4","topic":"Ephedrine & dopamine: indirect vasopressors","domain":"Anesthetic pharmacology (inhaled agents, IV induction agents, opioids, neuromuscular blocking agents, local anesthetics, vasopressors, reversal agents)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"Why does dobutamine require caution in patients with ischemic heart disease?","answer":"Dobutamine's positive inotropic effects increase myocardial oxygen demand; vasodilator therapy (nitroglycerin) may be a safer alternative in ischemic heart disease","rationale":"Increased contractility raises VO2 myocardial demand; in the setting of coronary stenosis, this can precipitate ischemia that would not occur with a vasodilator achieving similar CO improvement.","bloom":"analyze","source":[{"book":"Marino ICU Book","page":200}],"confusable_with":"dobutamine as safe in all cardiogenic shock patients"}
