  {"id":"epi-norepi-vasopressors-4","topic":"Epinephrine & norepinephrine: vasopressors","domain":"Anesthetic pharmacology (inhaled agents, IV induction agents, opioids, neuromuscular blocking agents, local anesthetics, vasopressors, reversal agents)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"What is the primary receptor effect of phenylephrine and its consequence for cardiac output?","answer":"Pure alpha-1 agonist causing peripheral vasoconstriction with a concomitant rise in SVR and arterial blood pressure — may reflexively reduce heart rate and cardiac output","rationale":"Without beta-1 activity, phenylephrine raises afterload without augmenting contractility; reflex bradycardia can reduce CO, limiting its utility in states with marginal cardiac reserve.","bloom":"analyze","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":380}],"confusable_with":"phenylephrine increasing cardiac output like ephedrine"}
