  {"id":"extubation-planning-4","topic":"Extubation planning and technique","domain":"Airway management (assessment & prediction, supraglottic & ETT devices, laryngoscopy & fiberoptic techniques, surgical airway)","discipline":"anesthesia","stem":"After thoracic surgery, what is the general extubation approach and why is early extubation preferred?","answer":"Extubate most patients shortly after surgery when standard criteria met, to decrease risk of pulmonary barotrauma (especially rupture of bronchial suture line); delay until criteria met in marginal reserve patients","rationale":"Positive-pressure ventilation can stress newly sutured bronchial or pulmonary anastomoses; early extubation to spontaneous breathing eliminates this mechanical stress.","bloom":"apply","source":[{"book":"Morgan & Mikhail","page":914}],"confusable_with":"prolonged ventilation after all thoracic procedures"},
